  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Monday)\nIssued at 1139 PM CDT MON SEP 7 2026\n\nThere are some lingering showers and isolated storms in the far\nsouthwest right now. These will dissipate or work into\nMississippi shortly after Midnight. The strong gradient from the\neasterly wedge is forecast to keep the boundary layer mixed, with\nsustained easterly winds of 5 to 10 mph. A few spots in the\nhigher elevations across the eastern and northeastern counties\nmay actually observe some wind gusts between 15 and 20 mph\novernight.\n\nThe easterly wedge will remain in place through Wednesday as the\n500 mb ridge builds aloft. Rain chances will remain minimal\nthrough Wednesday, generally limited to a 20-30% chance of\nshowers or storms along and south of I-85. While temperatures\nwill remain in the 90s, slightly drier air associated with the\neasterly wedge will keep conditions below Heat Advisory criteria.\nForecast confidence is increasing regarding the persistence of\nthe ridge through Thursday, with southerly moisture returning to\nthe region on Friday. Meanwhile, a cold front moving toward the\nOhio Valley is expected to lose upper support and stall to our\nnorthwest by Friday afternoon. This will lead to increased\ndiurnally-driven rain chances through the weekend, with highs\ncontinuing in the low to mid 90s.\n\n16",
